icle entitled the unexpected  struggle to make doctors allies in prep  jake sobo  said if you asked me what i thought the  biggest obstacles facing prep would be  it would not have immediately occurred  to me to add doctors to the list i would  have expected doctors to be an easy sell  the reality is precisely the opposite of  what i expected  um in a 2013 survey of 573 infectious  disease stocks throughout the u.s and  canada it was found that the majority  did in fact support prep  but only nine percent had actually  provided prep and about a third believed  that prep was irrelevant to their  practice  and this last point stood out to me  because of an experience that i had with  a good friend who lives in washington dc  and is  an hiv prevention researcher himself is  very well informed about prep and he's  in a stereo discordant relationship and  wanted to  get on prep um for the added layer of  protection he and his boyfriend use  condoms but he wanted to feel extra safe  and have this option  so he called me up one day incredibly  frustrated because he was trying to get  prep and couldn't get it despite the  fact he had health insurance when he  went to his primary care physician  the doctor was unfamiliar with prep and  referred him to an infectious disease  doctor then he called up several  different infectious disease doctors and  they all refused to give him an  appointment because he did not in fact  have an infectious disease  so he fortunately was able to get into a  clinical trial and is now on prep and  having a good experience with it  but not everybody is as fortunate as he  was to be living in a city where  clinical trials are going on  so you can see that many people may be  facing this problem where  they aren't able to get a prescription  from their doctor even if they have  the finances for even if they have  insurance  i think it's important to keep in mind  that if providers aren't willing to  prescribe prep  people may get it by other means 