rn is if a man is prescribed  prep he will then  go and have more unprotected sex or  increase his number of partners and  essentially the prep prescription will  do more harm than good  so far there has not been substantial  evidence for risk compensation occurring  in prep  in fact several trials have reported  decreases in risk behavior across the  duration of the study  that being said our evidence comes from  clinical trials or open label extensions  thereof and therefore they're not  representative of the real world so in  other words people who are in the  clinical trials they don't know if  they're  on the placebo or on prep and especially  in the early  trials they were unaware of any efficacy  of prep so they would probably be less  likely to increase their risk behavior  because they might not have that  perceived decrease in susceptibility  so what we know so far is based on  people who have been in clinical trials  and within the clinical trials have  gotten very comprehensive hiv prevention  services  which although may be recommended for  real world  implementation as well in reality  people may not have access to that  comprehensive of a prevention package so  therefore these individuals are not  representative of real world individuals  so we don't yet know really what risk  compensation is going to look like  once prep is out there in the real world  we're still waiting on effectiveness  data  another concern is that  patients will acquire other stis so  along the same lines if they start using  prep and reduce their condom use  they may be putting themselves at risk  for stis other than hiv  um adherence is also a big concern  um  so  there's concern that people will be  inconsistent about adherence and then  you know susceptible to hiv but  in addition there's this added issue of  viral resistance  in that because the medication that's  used for prep is also the same  medication used to treat hiv there's  concern that individuals  will  acquire hiv may be sort of  inconsistently adherent acquire hiv  without knowing it and then continue to  be sort of inconsistently adherent and  in the process develop drug resistance  or  resistance to  the virus would be resistant to the  medication  there's also concern that they may be  more likely to select for um they may be  more likely to acquire drug resistance  strains although there hasn't been  evidence of this  in terms of the data so far resistance  has been extremely rare  there have been some developments of  viral resistance but i think altogether  it was about five people across all four  trials that i covered  okay  another issue that comes up is cost and  reimbursement  and  so far fingers crossed most insurance  companies and state medicaid programs  have been covering prep  and also gilead has a medication  assistance program for those people who  are low income they will provide prep  for free so  hopefully cost won't be too much  interference in  writing these prescriptions  another big one is drug toxicity and  side effects and you know doctors feel  like why would we be prescribing these  medications that could cause side  effects to perfectly healthy individuals  especially if there are other means of  prevention  i mean the argument is that there are  other medications that are prescribed to  healthy individuals for preventive  reasons that do have side effects  but beyond that  the side effects have been pretty  minimal for  truvada is prep so  there's been basically a comparable  level of serious adverse events between  placebo groups and treatment groups  within clinical trials  the primary side effect that is seen  with truvada is gi issues such as nausea  however that's seen in a minority it's  usually just for the first month or so  and dissipates so it is pretty mild  there's also been reported a one percent  reduction in bone mineral density  and there's concern about renal  complications although renal  complications haven't been  highly supported by the clinical trials  i think that concern comes more from  looking at truvada within people who are  hiv positive  okay um so safety and efficacy and lack  of real world evidence are another  concern you know right now we have the  clinical trial evidence that i presented  but right now we don't really know what  it's going to look like in the real  world and so this is a concern for  providers  they're also  unwilling to prescribe because of a lack  of personal knowledge  um  and so i think that you know it's up to  a provider to be knowledgeable about the  prevention options that are out there  but also for those of us who are in  public health i think we have a role in  educating providers about prep  in order to increase their knowledge  resources have also come up and so  this could be in a couple of different  ways so some providers feel like they  simply don't have  the  time personally to be monitoring these  patients so closely and sitting down and  having these adherence counseling or  risk counseling sessions or they  wouldn't have the the staff to do it  and also resources have been expressed  as a concern in terms of  resource allocation  so using a particular medication for  preventative versus treatment purposes  if there's a limited supply in resource  limited senate  settings  and finally  there's also concerns about needs so  some providers simply feel like there  are other alternatives good alternatives  that work  there's no need for prep  and also there's debate around risk  level  you know at what level of risk should a  person really be prescribed prep  so now i'd like to turn to some of my  recent research  so what i'll be presenting today is a  pilot study that i conducted with my  colleagues here at yale  entitled the impact of patient race on  clinical decisions related to  prescribing hiv pre-exposure prophylaxis  assumptions about sexual risk  compensation and implications for  access um so first it's important i  think just to kind of set the stage for  this work to note the social disparities  in hiv prevalence  racial and sexual minority groups  continue to be disproportionately  affected  so  black men represent less than 14 percent  of the us male population and yet they  account for 38 percent of  u.s men living with hiv  we know that black msm in particular  bear the brunt of the burden among black  men so even though black msm account for  probably  two to five percent of the black male  population they account for almost  three-quarters of all newly acquired hiv  infections  we know that establishing effective and  accessible hiv prevention strategies for  communities of high preference is a  national health priority  therefore it's important to look at prep  and the potential benefit that it might  offer to some of these minority  communities where there is a high  prevalence of hiv  so while prep is indeed an exciting new  strategy for hiv prevention  the real world effectiveness of it will  depend critically upon provider uptake  and appropriate implementation within  clinical practice  and for multiple reasons prep might be  especially vulnerable to discriminatory  prescription practices  first there's documentation of racial  disparities in patient diagnosis and  treatment  across medical conditions so this is  pervasive throughout health care and  hiv care is no exception so multiple  studies have shown that black patients  experience longer delays to receiving  antiretroviral medication when in care  this could be foretelling of race-based  differences in receipt of antiretroviral  medication for preventive purposes in  other words prep  we also know that greater reliance on  provider discretion as opposed to clear  clinical criteria increases the risk of  discrimination occurring  so research has shown that racial  discrimination is more likely in  contexts in which  behavioral norms or situational demands  are ambiguous and that this applies to  medical settings  so as mentioned previously right now um  clear eligible criteria clear  eligibility criteria are lacking um so  the cdc has put out this  guidance but it's labeled interim  guidance as they work on more  comprehensive  guidelines for providers  and so right now  you know they they say that someone  needs to be at very high risk  they offer an example or two but they  don't really give specifics  the maker of the medication itself  gilead  as well as some other sources have tried  to offer more specifics but for the most  part  who is eligible and who is not and who  qualifies is really dependent on the  provider's discretion  and we know that provider opinions about  hypothetical cases and standards are  conflicting so there has been research  that has presented providers with these  different cases and we we indeed see  that there's controversy controversy  among them  so as mentioned previously one concern  that's been repeatedly expressed as a  deterrent to prescribing prep is sexual  risk compensation  to date there's  really no substantial evidence for  sexual risk compensation with prep  there's  no evidence for racial disparities in  sexual risk compensation so  there's no reason to believe that  a black man or a black  gay or bisexual man would engage in more  sexual risk than  a white man or a white man who has sex  with men  nonetheless we know that there are these  sexual stereotypes out there of black  men and  black msm in particular as being  sexually risky and out of control  importantly there's no empirical basis  for this not just in terms of prep  research but also  you know if you look at sexual risk  taking in general in the msm community  we know that black msm  do not engage in more risk behavior than  their other race counterparts in fact in  many ways they're a bit better about  condom use  or so less risky than other racial  groups  nonetheless because we do know that  there are these stereotypes  it's possible that stereotypes could  influence clinical judgment  okay so  i have  within my program of research i have  several overarching research goals so  first based on this background i gave  you  i want to identify disparities in  clinical judgments about prep based on  patient race as well as other patient  characteristics so things like sexual  orientation and gender  the potential risk behavior whether it's  sexual or drug use  and so on  and i want to look at how these  different factors affect predictions  about sexual risk compensation among  providers as well as providers  willingness to prescribe prep  the goal of this research beyond that is  to understand the psychological  mechanisms that underlie disparities in  judgment in order to  address the disparities through provider  targeted educational interventions  so today what i'll be focusing on is the  pilot study  which addresses the first goal  the latter two goals are part of a  planned program of research that i've  mapped out over the next few years  so  in the pilot study that i'll be  presenting my colleagues and i chose to  look at medical students in particular  as healthcare providers  because they are providers in training  they in theory have many years of  service ahead of them  and  we we thought that they may be more  likely to draw on their formal medical  education as opposed to clinic  background clinical experience  so my colleagues and i approached the  study with two objectives in mind  first to examine the predictions about  sexual risk compensation for a black man  who has sex with men as opposed to a  white man who has sex with men  and um  in in particular we're gonna we wanted  to look at it in the context of  a primary care scenario where the person  was actually seeking out prep  we also wanted to test the indirect  effect of patient race on prep  prescription willingness through sexual  risk compensation so specifically we  hypothesized that  the black patient would be judged as  more likely to increase  his sexual risk taking if prescribed  prep and that this in turn would lead to  a reduced willingness to prescribe prep  to the patient  okay so we tested these objectives using  an online survey in which participants  were presented with a vignette  describing  a patient who is seeking prep  in a primary care scenario and then they  were asked to make clinical decisions  about that patient  so patient race was systematically  manipulated such that participants were  randomized to one of two conditions  describing either a black patient or a  white patient  asking for prep in this medical scenario  so an initial and follow-up email  request for participation in an  anonymous online survey about attitudes  towards prescribing prep was distributed  to the yale medical student listserv  our final sample was comprised of 102  medical students  so the survey itself consisted of four  parts  um first participants were provided with  background information about truvada  including its purpose uh partial  efficacy according to the three clinical  trials that had taken place up until  then and its approval by the fda  second they were presented with quotes  representing popular arguments for and  against the prescription of prep  then the clinical vignette itself was  displayed which i'll show you in just a  moment  and finally participants were asked to  make a series of clinical judgments  pertaining to the vignette and to  respond to other survey items  so here's the vignette that the  participants were shown i've highlighted  key purpose or key pieces of information  just for the purpose of this talk  so the vignette read as follows mr j is  a 31 year old  black man who's hiv negative he presents  to you his primary care physician  stating that he wants to take the  medication to help prevent himself from  getting hiv  he has insurance that would cover the  prescription  mr j currently has one male sex partner  who has been diagnosed as hiv positive  and with whom he is monogamous  during previous appointments you've  discussed you've discussed hiv risk with  him and encouraged him to use condoms  however he does not always use them  resulting in repeated episodes of  unprotected sex with his partner  hiv antibody and rna lab tests confirmed  that mr j is hiv negative he's never had  any stds he has no physical complaints  he's never had surgery or been  hospitalized his medical history is  otherwise unremarkable  mr j does not use alcohol tobacco or  other drugs he has no known drug  allergies and is not currently taking  any medication  so after participants were shown that  vignette they were asked a series of  questions  they were asked about predicted sexual  risk compensation so specifically we  asked how likely would this patient be  to have more unprotected sex if he  started taking truvada as prep  we asked about prep prescription  willingness specifically asking would  you prescribe truvada as prep to this  patient  and we also asked about other clinical  judgments things like predicted  adherence  risk of the patient's risk of hiv  infection if they were not to prescribe  prep  the risk reduction that they associated  with prep  we asked about racial bias as well  which we conceptualized as perceived  importance of the patient's request as  well as general feelings towards black  patients versus white patients  and finally we asked about background  characteristics including both  sociodemographics as well as medical  training characteristics  so univariate analyses of covariance  controlling for participant race were  used to compare clinical judgments  between patient race conditions  we used hierarchical linear regression  in order to  look at predictors of prescription  willingness  and finally we tested for the indirect  effect that i showed you in the diagram  earlier using bootstrapping  moving on to the results  in terms of sample characteristics  participants were evenly split by gender  the two largest racial groups  represented were white and asian there  were very few latinos blacks or people  of other races  the majority of participants identified  as heterosexual  and students from all years of medical  school were represented  notably none of these characteristics  differed significantly across conditions  so our first goal was to  examine the effect of patient race on  predictions about sexual risk  compensation  so this table here displays means  comparisons between conditions in terms  of clinical judgments of the black  patient which is condition one  versus clinical judgments of the white  patient which is condition two  as you can see along the left column  these are the different different  clinical judgments that were examined  and by looking at the p values you can  see that the one that emerged as  significantly different was patient  sexual risk compensation  so you can see that the black patient  was judged as more likely to engage in  sexual risk compensation than the white  patient next we sought to examine the  effect of predicted sexual risk  compensation on willingness to prescribe  prep  controlling for patient race  uh so this table shows results from our  our hierarchical linear regression  the independent variables are listed in  the left column  and the lines represent the  three different sets of variables  that were  entered i want to draw your attention to  the variable at the bottom which is  predicted patient sexual risk  compensation which was added by itself  in the third and final step  i'll blow this up for you  so you can see that predicted patient  sexual risk compensation  was highly significantly related to  prescription willingness  overall our model accounted for 32  percent of the variance in willingness  to prescribe prep  and the addition of that one variable to  the model accounted for an additional 11  percent of the variance  so finally we sought to test the entire  model testing the indirect effect of  patient race on prep prescription  willingness through predicted sexual  risk compensation  we used bootstrapping to calculate a  bias corrected and accelerated 95  confidence interval of the indirect  effect  and as can be seen a significant  indirect effect emerged  as indicated by a confidence interval  that did not straddle zero  so turning to the model you can see the  unstandardized coefficients and standard  errors  for all of the paths  and  to summarize these  results indicated that the black patient  was judged as more likely to engage in  sexual risk compensation which in turn  was related to reduced prescription  willingness  so in summary the black patient was  judged as more likely to engage in  sexual risk compensation than the white  patient  and keep in mind in this particular  scenario the hypothetical patient was  kind of a straight edge you know he was  kind of a goody two shoes he didn't  drink he didn't smoke or do drugs and  importantly he was monogamous  some might say he was the ideal  candidate for prep and yet despite the  fact that he did not fit the black male  stereotype of being sexually out of  control or hypersexual by description we  still found this difference  we also found that predicted patient  sexual risk compensation was strongly  associated associated with prep  prescription willingness  over and above sociodemographic and  medical training characteristics racial  bias and other forms of clinical  judgment  patient race was indirectly related to  prescription willingness such that the  black patient  was judged as more likely to engage in  sexual risk and this was linked to  reduced willingness to prescribe  okay so in terms of future research some  of which i alluded to and sharing my  research goals  i think it's important to look at other  healthcare provider samples i think it's  very interesting  to focus on the medical student  community but we also want to know what  biases exist among people who are  actively prescribing  and actively practicing presently  also we want to look beyond just  we want to look in other clinical  settings if ideally prep is going to be  implemented across a broad spectrum of  clinical settings we want to find out  what biases and discriminatory practices  might occur in those settings and to  look  throughout  throughout the country  i'm also interested in looking at  moderators  things like provider characteristics in  particular provider race  it's been shown previously that  provider race and the concordance  between provider race and patient race  is related to  decisions around hiv treatment so i  think it's interesting to look at that  in the future unfortunately we just  didn't have the numbers to look at it  with this sample  also i want to look at other patient  characteristics including gender and  sexual orientation  and risk behavior  i also want to explore psychological  mechanisms so looking at the implicit  and or explicit biases and prejudice  they might be underlying these clinical  decisions  and finally it's important to look at  this in real world prescription behavior  right now this is just within the  context of a clinical vignette we want  to see if this is actually happening as  prep is rolled out  this research has implications for  future intervention as well first it  speaks to  the potential or the need to increase  awareness about prep among providers and  to prevent stereotypes from adversely  affecting clinical judgment  there are several different  approaches to  doing this that have been put forward in  the field of social cognitive psychology  including things like emphasizing  individuation or focusing on individual  characteristics as opposed to social  categorization or group membership  also building a sense of partnership  between the patient and the provider and  informing providers about their own  vulnerability to stereotyping and  discrimination  so prep is a promising prevention  strategy hopefully i've convinced you of  that  i think it has great potential to impact  the hiv epidemic  but whether you're on board with it or  not at this point i think we can all  agree that if it is going to be  prescribed it should be prescribed  fairly and people across the social  spectrum should have equal access to it  therefore stereotypes and biases should  not be allowed to become bases for  denying access  black msm which is the group that i  focused on in my research they comprise  one of many different socially  marginalized groups who stand to benefit  from prep  other social groups who are considered  to be at heightened risk based on  their socio-demographic characteristics  or their behavioral practices so groups  like transgender individuals or sex  workers they also might confront  healthcare bias that interferes with  access to prep so it's important to  consider other groups as well  so far in hearing about  kind of barriers to equitable access  most of what i've heard about is things  like cost and resource allocation but we  really need to consider  other factors as well not just  structural  i think it's essential that we start  having honest conversations about race  and how race and how stereotypes play  into clinical decision making  in presenting this work my  purpose is not to or my intention is not  to name call or point fingers at  healthcare providers most are very well  intentioned who are just individuals who  are just trying to maximize the health  of their patients  however like it or not healthcare  providers  and everyone else in this room are  members of a society where stereotypes  still exist and stereotypes influence  behavior and sometimes unconsciously  therefore we can't ignore the potential  influence of stereotypes on treatment in  the health care system and related to  prep specifically  what i also want to drive home is that  timing is critical for increasing  awareness about stereotypes and their  potential impact on prep related  clinical judgments  right now is when  prep is being introduced to healthcare  settings across the country right now is  when the cdc is putting together these  more comprehensive guidelines so i think  it could be valuable to integrate some  of this material  into these introductions and into these  guidelines  finally i want to acknowledge my  co-authors some of whom are in the room  as well as  the yale university interdisciplinary  hiv prevention training program the t32  program of which i'm a part  sierra  dr jamie meyer who's in the audience and  helped us to refine our clinical  vignette dr nancy ingoth who helped with  recruitment and the new haven restaurant  owners who  donated gift certificates to serve as  participant incentives  finally i want to thank the medical  student participants  who made this research happen  i also want to put in a quick plug  for those of you who are interested in  discussion and debate and or research on  the topic of prep  dr margaret weeks is uh forming a  prep interest group  and  you are welcome to contact her at this  address or if you want to reach out to  me i will put you in touch with her
 

